          Fragment:md
            ## Vite Powers Most Integrations

            You're probably already using nmbl through Vite. The **Vue**, **React**,
            and **Solid** guides all add the nmbl Vite plugin to a standard Vite
            config (before the framework plugin), and **Astro** runs on Vite too.
            In those setups the plugin preprocesses framework templates.

            This guide covers using `@nmbl-lang/vite-plugin` directly for the
            generic case: loading **standalone `.nmbl` files** as compiled HTML,
            with no framework involved — handy for static pages, partials, email
            templates, or anywhere you just want markup.

            ## Installation

            ```bash
            # npm
            npm install --save-dev @nmbl-lang/vite-plugin

            # bun
            bun add -d @nmbl-lang/vite-plugin
            ```

            ## Configuration

            Add the plugin to your Vite config. For standalone `.nmbl` files it
            defaults to the `html` target (plain markup — no framework needed):

            ```ts title="vite.config.ts"
            import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
            import nmbl from '@nmbl-lang/vite-plugin';

            export default defineConfig({
              plugins: [nmbl()],
            });
            ```

            When you pair it with a framework, add `nmbl()` **before** that
            framework's plugin so it preprocesses templates first — exactly what
            the other guides do:

            ```ts title="vite.config.ts"
            plugins: [
              nmbl({ jsx: { framework: 'react' } }), // before react()
              react(),
            ]
            ```

            ## Loading a File

            Importing a `.nmbl` file gives you the compiled HTML string as the
            default export:

            ```nmbl title="card.nmbl"
            section.card
              h1 Hello from nmbl
              p.muted Compiled to HTML at build time.
            ```

            ```ts title="main.ts"
            import html from './card.nmbl';

            document.querySelector('#app').innerHTML = html;
            ```

            Edit the `.nmbl` file and Vite's HMR updates the page instantly.

            ## Frontmatter

            A `.nmbl` file can carry YAML frontmatter, exported alongside the
            markup as `frontmatter`:

            ```nmbl title="page.nmbl"
            ---
            title: Welcome
            description: A standalone nmbl page.
            ---
            article.card
              h1 Welcome
              p A page compiled from nmbl.
            ```

            ```ts title="main.ts"
            import html, { frontmatter } from './page.nmbl';

            document.title = frontmatter.title;
            document.querySelector('#app').innerHTML = html;
            ```

            ## Markdown Blocks

            The plugin registers the built-in CommonMark + GFM filter, so `:md`
            content blocks work out of the box in standalone files:

            ```nmbl title="docs.nmbl"
            article.prose:md
              ## Getting started

              Prose with **bold**, `code`, and [links](/). Blank lines separate
              paragraphs, and raw inline HTML works too.
            ```

            ## TypeScript

            Tell TypeScript about the `.nmbl` import shape with an ambient
            declaration somewhere in your project:

            ```ts title="nmbl.d.ts"
            declare module '*.nmbl' {
              const html: string;
              export default html;
              export const frontmatter: Record<string, unknown>;
            }
            ```

